\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Corollary}
\title{Counting isometries preserve volume}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{A linear isomorphism #{T:X_V\to X_W} preserving the counting inner
products has \vocabk{reference-volume Jacobian}{fdip-00JI}
#{J(T)=1}. It therefore preserves coordinate Lebesgue
measure and the integrals in \ref{fdip-002K}. An isometric embedding
into a higher-dimensional space is not included.}
\proof{
\p{Order the source indicators as #{e_1,\ldots,e_n} and the target
indicators as #{f_1,\ldots,f_n}. Both bases are orthonormal, as is
#{Te_1,\ldots,Te_n}, since #{T} is an isometric isomorphism.
Use the corresponding normalized top forms to obtain the multiplier
#{d(T)} from the proof of \ref{fdip-002K}. On simple top
exterior vectors the induced inner product is
##{\left\langle u_1\wedge\cdots\wedge u_n,
 v_1\wedge\cdots\wedge v_n\right\rangle
 =\det\bigl(h(u_i,v_j)\bigr).}
Alternating multilinearity makes this well-defined on the top exterior
power. The wedge of an ordered orthonormal basis has squared norm one,
and an isometry preserves every entry of the displayed Gram matrix.
The identity
##{Te_1\wedge\cdots\wedge Te_n
 =d(T)f_1\wedge\cdots\wedge f_n}
therefore gives #{d(T)^2=1}, so #{J(T)=1}; apply \ref{fdip-002K}.
The empty wedge also has norm one.}
}
\p{This is a direct consequence of the preceding transformation theorem,
with the convention of
\citet{Exercise 1.2.21, p. 41, preliminary version}{tao2011measure}.
Mathlib's \leanref{LinearIsometryEquiv.measurePreserving} concerns
finite-dimensional real inner-product spaces with compatible Borel
structures and their canonical volumes. An arbitrary perfect pairing
or a general quadratic-form isometry does not supply these hypotheses.}
